          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which drug classes does heart failure guidance describe for reduced ejection fraction?",
          "answer": "Current heart failure guidance describes four foundational drug classes for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (EF 40% or below). The source is the heart failure consensus summary, \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes.\" It says these are started together or in quick succession, not added one at a time over months:\n\n1. **Renin-angiotensin system blockade:** an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor where suitable, otherwise an ACE inhibitor, otherwise an angiotensin receptor blocker.\n2. **Beta-blocker:** one with evidence specifically in heart failure. It is started when the person is not congested, because starting during congestion can worsen it.\n3. **Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist:** potassium and kidney function are monitored.\n4. **SGLT-2 inhibitor:** used for this indication whether or not diabetes is present.\n\n**Evidence strength:** The retrieved passages do not state a class of recommendation or level of evidence for any of these classes, so I can't grade them from this material. Ask a clinician or check the full guideline for those grades.\n\n**Disagreements:** The heart failure document reports no disagreement section, and I found no conflicting heart failure guidance in these results. The disagreements that did come up concern hypertension and osteoarthritis, which are outside this question.\n\nDoses and which medicines suit a particular person are decisions for a clinician who knows the full history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
